- [The Command Bar](https://www.sketch.com/docs/interface-and-settings/the-mac-app-interface/the-command-bar/): The Command Bar gives you quick keyboard access to almost any action you’d find in the menu bar, including those from plugins. Over time, the Command Bar will learn your preferences and bring the actions you use regularly to the top of the results.
- [The Toolbar](https://www.sketch.com/docs/interface-and-settings/the-mac-app-interface/the-toolbar/): The toolbar gives you quick access to the tools and actions you use most in the Mac app. It’s contextual, meaning it automatically updates to show options that are relevant to your current selection and what you’re working on.
- [The Inspector](https://www.sketch.com/docs/interface-and-settings/the-mac-app-interface/the-inspector/): In the Inspector, you’ll find settings for any layers you’ve selected, or controls for the tool you’re using. The Inspector hides and shows these controls and settings based on what you have selected or what you’re doing.
- [The Layer List](https://www.sketch.com/docs/interface-and-settings/the-mac-app-interface/the-layer-list/): In the layer list, you’ll find your open documents, pages, frames, and layers. You can reorder and rename them directly from here.
- [The Components View](https://www.sketch.com/docs/interface-and-settings/the-mac-app-interface/the-components-view/): The Components View is a quick and easy way to create, manage, search, and find local Symbols, Text Styles, Layer Styles, Color Variables, Frame Templates, and Graphic Templates in your document.
- [Mac app settings](https://www.sketch.com/docs/interface-and-settings/mac-app-settings/): Learn how to change Canvas settings and presets; add Libraries, data sources or plugins; enable experimental features — and more. Press <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>,</kbd> from anywhere in the Mac app, or choose **Sketch > Settings** to bring up the Settings panel.
