- [Symbols and Styles](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/): Styles are a means to change your design components in just about any way you like. Similarly, Symbols are reusable components that you can easily apply across all your designs.
In this section you’ll discover how styling can bring your designs to life, and how Symbols can optimize your workflow.
- [Styling](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/): You can add and edit style properties in the Inspector for layers like shapes and text, as well as containers like Frames and Graphics.
- [Corners](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/corners/): Learn how to adjust corner radius and styles in Sketch, including individual corners, corner types, and Auto corners.
- [Fills](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/fills/): Add solid colors, gradients, images, and Color Variables as fills to your layers and frames. Apply multiple fills, adjust opacity, and swap fills and borders.
- [The Color Panel](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/the-color-panel/)
- [Gradients](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/gradients/): Gradients blend two or more colors across a layer. There are three types: Linear, Radial, and Angular.
- [Tints](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/tints/): Learn how to apply Tints in Sketch with the help of our step-by-step guides and video tutorials.
- [Borders](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/borders/): Add borders to any layer in Sketch and customize their position, width, fill, and more — including per-side borders and gradient borders.
- [Shadows](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/shadows/): Shadows and Inner Shadows appear as separate sections in the Inspector. They have the same controls but work in different ways — Shadows render outside a layer, while Inner Shadows render inside.
- [Effects](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/effects/): Effects add depth, atmosphere, and movement to your layers. You can use Glass, Fade, Layer blur, Background blur, Motion blur, and Zoom blur — each with different visual results and controls.
- [Glass](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/effects/glass/): Glass softly blurs content behind a layer and lets background colors show through. Use Auto for Apple platforms or Custom to fine-tune every parameter.
- [Fade](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/effects/fade/): Fade gradually reduces a layer’s opacity from opaque to transparent — choose Linear for a directional fade or Radial for one that spreads outward from a central point.
- [Layer and Background Blur](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/effects/layer-and-background/): Apply blur to a layer’s own content or to everything behind it. Layer blur and Background blur both come in Uniform, Linear Progressive, and Radial Progressive styles.
- [Motion and Zoom Blur](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/effects/motion-and-zoom/): Motion blur blurs a layer in one direction to imply movement. Zoom blur radiates outward from a point to create a zoom or focus effect.
- [Blurs](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/blurs/): To add a blur, click the **Blur** section in the Inspector, then choose a blur type from the drop-down menu. To switch an existing blur to a different type, click the type name in the blur row or right-click it and choose from the menu.
- [Managing style properties](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/managing-style-properties/): Learn how to reorder, enable, remove, and copy style properties like fills, borders, and shadows between layers in Sketch.
- [Organizing Styles](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/styling/organizing-styles/)
- [Text and layer Styles](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/text-and-layer-styles/): When you select a layer in the Mac app, you can create and edit its style properties in the Inspector.
- [Text Styles](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/text-and-layer-styles/text-styles/): Our Text Styles tool makes styling your text easy. Store a set of tools and reuse them across different text layers within your designs.
- [Layer Styles](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/text-and-layer-styles/layer-styles/): Layer Styles work in exactly the same way as Text Styles. The only difference is that they work with shape layers, while Text Styles work with text layers.
- [Color Variables](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/color-variables/): Learn how to create, apply, and manage Color Variables in Sketch to keep colors consistent across your document and Libraries.
- [Selection colors](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/selection-colors/): Use the Colors section in the Inspector to view and swap colors across a selection of layers with different colors — whether you’re working with Symbol instances, plain layers, or a mix of both.
- [Symbols](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/symbols/): Learn how to create and edit Symbols in Sketch with the help of our step-by-step guides and video tutorials.
- [Overrides](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/overrides/): Customize Symbol instances using overrides for colors, text, images, Styles, and nested Symbols.
- [Replacing Components](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/replacing/): To replace a Component, select it on the Canvas and click its name in the Inspector. Use the arrow keys in the popover to select the Component you want to replace it with, then press <kbd>⏎</kbd>. Or, click a Component’s name or preview to replace it instantly.
- [Templates](https://www.sketch.com/docs/symbols-and-styles/templates/): Templates  are a great way to start a new project — rather than starting with a blank canvas you can start with a document that already includes frames, placeholder content and more. They’re also a great way to give everyone a head start on repeatable work, while improving consistency across your design documents.
By default Sketch includes templates with [ready-to-go designs](/profile/sketch-templates/) for presentations, social posts, mockups, business cards and so much more.
You can also turn any of your own documents into templates, which will make them available for anyone in your Workspace.
